the new program putting teenagers in the  shoes of military cadets for the goal it  uses exercise and character development  to teach them how to be more accountable  for themselves mary jane billy is  joining us live in the newsroom to  explain good morning mary jane good  morning heather one local mom says this  program is not only about giving your  kids a second chance but parents a  second chance too the battleborn youth  challenge academy is spearheaded by the  nevada national guard with the goal of  improving life skills and employment  potential for teens who have fallen  behind on school credits it's a  six-month program that is tuition-free  and teens live on campus and use a  quasi-military structure style to teach  the teens accountability discipline and  so much more parent nicole zamora says  while the program takes place up in  northern nevada she wants to raise  awareness of how beneficial this program  is and says her daughter esmeralda even  graduated at the top of her class last  month  it is a voluntary program so the  children have to be willing to go and i  talked to her about it i explained to  her what i was mentioning earlier like i  think you deserve the opportunity to be  the best you that you can be  and this will give you a chance to just  get away from all the distractions not  even worrying about me and what i have  going on in my work schedule and your  sister and sports and  just life in general  now zamora says while the camp isn't  easy her daughter came back focused and  motivated and not only helped her  daughter physically but mentally and  even academically as well zamora says  you can reach out to her on the email  provided below if you'd like to learn  more about her and her daughter's  experience with the battleborn youth  camp reporting live in the newsroom mary  jane boyzen live local now
 

